Orrery Glide

Ingredients: ELECTRO SWING + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Synth Lead
Creative Catalyst: Musical instruments hammered from extraterrestrial meteorite fragments and interlinked with 18th-century antique clockwork escapements.

Orrery Glide strips electro swing of its carnival brass and frenetic four-on-the-floor clatter, dissolving vintage 1920s syncopation into a weightless, oceanic fog. Suspended between antique horology and interstellar mineralogy, the sound balances the relentless, micro-shuffled mechanical precision of clock escapements against weeping, floating synth leads routed through crystallized meteorite stones.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 82–98 BPM. The bounce is not a stomping kick drum, but a delicate, micro-swung half-time lilt. Ticking balance wheels, falling escapement levers, and soft pneumatic velvet thuds provide a rhythmic lattice that feels both mathematically rigid and lazily adrift.
- Harmonic Palette: Gauzy French impressionist harmony meets celestial whole-tone drifts. Nostalgic jazz-age minor ninths and major-seventh suspensions are voiced on airy, detuned reeds, constantly blurred by suspended reverb tails that refuse to decay.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Pallasite Escapement Convolution: Digital audio signals are re-amped through transducers clamped directly to sliced olivine-iron meteorites, imparting an otherworldly metallic ring to every transient.
  2. Soporific Time-Stretch Scraping: 78 RPM shellac recordings of swing clarinets and flappers are stretched 800% into misty, continuous ambient pads that retain faint ghosts of vintage needle noise.
  3. Dead-Weight Ducking: Bass frequencies do not sidechain to a kick; instead, they duck whenever physical clockwork escapements click, creating a slow, tidal breathing effect.
- Dynamics: Songs don't drop; they drift into alignment. Tracks typically open in floating, weightless suspension before physical clock gears catch each other, locking the dreamy atmosphere into an intoxicating, hypnotic swing that slowly dissolves back into sea-spray mist.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged in the salvage drydocks of Saint-Malo, Brittany, after an antique dealer and an astrophysicist began wiring fragments of the 1892 Morbihan iron meteorite into damaged Breguet pocket-watch mechanisms. Disillusioned jazz buskers and harbor recluses gathered in abandoned tidal mills, crafting a subculture called Les Horlogers Célestes. Dressed in verdigris-dusted linen waistcoats, oilskin mariner capes, and jeweler loupes retrofitted with dark star-sapphire lenses, practitioners play late-night gatherings timed strictly to the spring tides. Before each performance, musicians rub meteorite dust between their fingertips and the brass keys, a ritual supposed to ground the electric static of the upper atmosphere into the swinging clockworks beneath their palms.

Typical Instruments: Meteorite-core monophonic synthesizer (using extraterrestrial iron alloy as variable inductive filters for weeping leads), modified antique clockwork percussion racks (springs, balance wheels, verge-and-foliot escapements), stretched shellac gramophone sampler, felted upright piano, celestial bowed saw.

